   "text": "I read the molecule as a DBCO\u2013PEG4\u2013Val\u2013Cit\u2013PAB linker carrying a tubulysin-type payload, and wrote the SMILES fragment by fragment.\n\n**Linker end**\n- DBCO: the strained dibenzo-azacyclooctyne, with N attached to a CH2 and to an aryl ring, and the alkyne bridging the two benzo rings.\n- The DBCO nitrogen carries a succinamide (C(=O)CCC(=O)N) leading into the PEG4 chain.\n- The PEG4 chain is NH\u2013CH2CH2\u2013(OCH2CH2)\u2084\u2013C(=O), with four ether oxygens counted in the drawing.\n- Next come L-Val and L-citrulline (ureido side chain), then a p-aminobenzyl carbamate (PAB) attached through O\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH to the aryl ring of the payload.\n\n**Payload**\n- A tubuphenylalanine-type unit carries a gem-dimethyl carboxylic acid; its amine-bearing carbon is the stereocentre.\n- It is amide-linked to a thiazole-4-carboxamide. The thiazole bears a CH(OAc) centre, then CH2, then a CH(iPr) centre.\n- That CH(iPr) centre carries an N-(pent-4-ynyloxy) tertiary amide nitrogen, written N(OCCCC#C).\n- The chain ends in isoleucine and N-methylpipecolic acid (Mep).\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- Val and Cit are L.\n- The OAc carbon and the CH(iPr)N carbon are both R, which matches the wedges as I read them.\n- The tubuphenylalanine carbon was set from the wedged N bond.\n- Ile is taken as the natural L-Ile (2S,3S), and Mep as the R (D) configuration. I assumed these two from the natural tubulysin series rather than reading every wedge and hash in the drawing.\n\nSMILES: O=C(CCC(=O)N1Cc2ccccc2C#Cc2ccccc12)NCCOCCOCCOCCOCCC(=O)N[C@@H](C(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCNC(N)=O)C(=O)Nc1ccc(COC(=O)Nc2ccc(C[C@@H](CC(C)(C)C(=O)O)NC(=O)c3csc(n3)[C@H](OC(C)=O)C[C@H](C(C)C)N(OCCCC#C)C(=O)[C@H]([C@@H](C)CC)NC(=O)[C@H]4CCCCN4C)cc2)cc1"
